72 #uuid "d7eb0065-e372-5f47-afb0-364ee5325cfa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120200 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301848 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120200 #edb/ref 1069 "q-32684" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120200 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120200 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose that a $3\\times 3$ matrix $C$ is reduced to row echelon form using the following elementary row operations, applied in the order in which they appear:\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+4{R}_{1}\n$$\n\nGiven that $C$ has the LU factorization $C=LU,$ then $L=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120200 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "80727ea8-1d37-5678-9a50-5d7e94ac9250" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120200 #edb/ref 1074 "Applying ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1)\\cdot {R}_{1}$ and then ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+4\\cdot {R}_{1}$ is equivalent to multiplying $C$ from the left by the following elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{2}}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{1}}\\cdot C=U\n$$\n\nNow, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}C=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}C & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}C & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ C & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ C & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ C & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ C & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot U\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186120656 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120657 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120656 #edb/ref 1061 17360 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120656 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d1731663-b6e6-5bf1-a62a-a1b6ab66b554" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120656 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121029 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120657 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120658 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120657 #edb/ref 1061 17361 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120657 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "02b00fc5-d232-5394-bd52-5c011f1db096" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120657 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119794 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120658 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120658 #edb/ref 1061 17362 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120658 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "214a0b2c-29b3-58b2-a7c5-0300d4430cab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120658 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119795 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120659 #edb/ref 1061 17364 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120659 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "dffaaec1-80eb-5582-bd94-3dde9c7b5f0c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120659 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119796 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121029 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that LU factorization involves writing a matrix $A$ as a product of two matrices $L$ and $U,$ where\n\n- ![](images/2e/2e6c0b6fdaf90e3a3e23ed91ffe09cb360bb39325b9a630f1b7219b3188a25db.svg) is a *unit lower triangular matrix* (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal), and\n\n- ![](images/0c/0c5d7337e47a53dfa5bdce8c6f4a96bb1e52ef6054349011a6cc38f29701cc61.svg) is an *upper triangular matrix*.\n\nFor example, suppose we're given the matrix ![](images/22/22dba4fe66ac5478f5d81a68342a01809a360de8e722e809998655e7740bafe8.svg) To perform LU factorization, we need to write $A$ as \n\n![](images/d4/d4c92b2e8252619f9a1511d166be884b9a2bdb4311eea8e408e8fe75eed6f36c.svg)\n\nAs usual, to find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we can reduce the matrix $A$ to *echelon form* while simultaneously performing the same elementary row operations on the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 7\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} & ⇔ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}=U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since $E_1A=U,$ we can invert $E_1$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Caution:** In order to get a unit lower triangular matrix at the end, we have the following restriction while reducing a matrix $A$ to its *echelon form*:\n\n- we can only add multiples of higher rows to lower rows ($R_j:=R_j+k R_i$ where $i < j$), and\n\n- the other elementary row operations (swapping or multiplying a row by a number) are not allowed." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186121029 #edb/ref 1065 3178 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121029 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "5f8c6994-f448-5679-8d2e-5591b86c2a97" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121029 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241189 #edb/ref 1080 "-6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241189 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241189 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "006f8bad-4488-5247-b030-e69c65d69aa9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241398 #edb/ref 1080 "4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241398 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241398 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "18cb4e22-4527-5cdf-a246-1015081dac6c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241811 #edb/ref 1080 "9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241811 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 1759